course: dictionary
lesson: 12852
title: Energise: to fill with energy
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Energise is a verb. It means to give someone or something energy, so they feel lively or start working. «A brisk walk energises me before school.» After a brisk walk you feel awake and full of go, so energise means to make you feel lively.
explain: The word has a second meaning in science. To energise a circuit means to send electricity through it so it works. «When Sam closed the switch, the current energised the circuit and the bulb glowed.» The bulb lights up because the circuit now has power.
explain: Try your meaning in the sentence. «The team talk energised the players» works if it means the players suddenly felt lively and keen. If your meaning makes the sentence silly, go back and check the clues around the word.
explain: Spelling check: British English uses energise with an s, and American English uses energize with a z. Both spellings mean the same thing.
fact: Energise is a verb, so its forms are energise, energises, energised and energising.
fact: «Energise» means to make someone feel full of energy, lively and keen.
fact: «Energise» can also mean to give electricity to something so it works.
fact: Food, exercise and music can all energise you.
fact: In British English the word is spelt energise; in American English it is spelt energize.
exercise: «A walk in the fresh air energises me.» What does energises mean? | Stops me from moving | *Makes me feel lively and full of energy | Makes the air colder | Makes me feel sleepy and slow
exercise: «The current energised the circuit, and the lamp lit up.» What does energised mean here? | Hid the circuit from view | Cut the wires in two | Painted the circuit a bright colour | *Gave electricity so the circuit worked
exercise: Which spelling is British English? | enegise | energyse | *energise | energize
test: «The team talk energised the players, and they scored twice.» What does energised mean? | *Made them feel lively and keen | Made them leave the pitch | Made them lose the game | Made them tired and quiet
test: Which sentence uses energise to mean giving electricity? | *«Closing the switch energised the circuit.» | «She energised the room with a joke.» | «Loud music energises the crowd.» | «Hot soup energises me on a cold day.»
exam: «Yesterday, the strong coffee ___ me before the test.» Which form fits the gap? | *energised | energises | energising | energise
